PLACES=[
N('fred-harvey-house-leavenworth-ks','Fred Harvey House','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.31161,-94.91672,'exact','view','drive','','1875–1901',
  'Fred Harvey, who built the chain of Harvey House railroad restaurants and hotels along the Santa Fe, made Leavenworth his home from 1865. The three-storey stone house was built in 1875 by A. A. Higginbotham; Harvey bought it in 1883 and died there on February 9, 1901. His family lived in it until 1943. It then became a nurses\' home, and in 1949 passed to the Board of Education.',
  'A large three-storey stone house in the old residential streets near downtown. What it is used for now was not checked, and neither was the interior.',
  'The house where the man who "civilized the West" with railroad dining lived and died.',
  'At 624 Olive Street. Private or institutional; look from the public street. Present owner and use not checked.',
  [['National Register form, Fred Harvey House (72000510)',NR%'72000510'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR]],brick='no'),
N('santa-fe-depot-leavenworth-ks','Santa Fe depot (The Depot restaurant)','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','rail','standing',39.31766,-94.92263,'exact','open','drive','','ca. 1887',
  'The Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe passenger depot at 781 Shawnee Street was built about 1887 in Romanesque Revival style from rusticated, dark pink sandstone, with gabled projections and a tower. When it was listed in 1986 it was vacant, with its openings boarded up, and the Santa Fe tracks beside it were abandoned. A travel article names it now as a restaurant called The Depot.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey sandstone station with arched doorways, broad eaves on large wooden brackets and a tower. The 1986 form says the slate roof tiles, metal cresting and tall brick chimneys were already gone.',
  'A pink sandstone Santa Fe station that now serves dinner.',
  'The Depot is a working restaurant, so it is open to customers at its own hours; its page was not read and hours are not checked. Street parking and lots by the building were not checked.',
  [['National Register form, Santa Fe depot (86001321)',NR%'86001321'],['Only In Your State: the depot restaurant','https://www.onlyinyourstate.com/kansas/kansas-train-depot-restaurant-leavenworth/'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR]],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('old-union-depot-leavenworth-ks','Old Union Depot (Riverfront Community Center)','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','rail','standing',39.31861,-94.90917,'exact','open','drive','','1888',
  'Built in 1888 as a Union Pacific depot by James A. McGonigle to a design by the Chicago firm of Cobb and Frost, it is a 200-foot-long Romanesque Revival building of brick with sandstone trim, set on a terrace between the Missouri River and the town. The city bought it in 1984 and it opened in 1988 as a recreation centre with a gym, pool, courts and an indoor track.',
  'The 1982 listing form describes red slate roofing, sandstone gables and corner posts, arched entrances and two double iron stairways down to the old boarding area, with the original walls still behind later office partitions. The inside has since been refitted for recreation.',
  'A big Romanesque river depot that is now the town\'s fitness centre.',
  'Run by the city of Leavenworth Parks and Recreation at 201 South Main Street. Wikipedia gives opening hours but the city page could not be read, so hours are not checked.',
  [W('Leavenworth Riverfront Community Center'),['National Register form, Old Union Depot (82002663)',NR%'82002663']],brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('terminal-railway-freight-depot-leavenworth-ks','Leavenworth Terminal Railway & Bridge Company freight depot','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','rail','standing',39.3165,-94.9182,'site','view','drive','','1893–1959',
  'Both parts of the Terminal Depot were built in 1893: a two-storey brick office block and a one-storey freight room with a shallow gable roof. It served the Leavenworth Terminal Railway and Bridge Company, whose rails crossed the Missouri River bridge. The National Register form dates its use from 1893 to 1959.',
  'A plain red brick office block with patterned brick in the cornice and darker courses making stripes, limestone sills, and a brick vault in the freight room; the windows and doors are modern replacements in the old openings.',
  'A red brick terminal depot of 1893 with a striped parapet.',
  'Look from the public street. Who owns it and what it is used for now was not checked.',
  [['National Register form, Terminal Railway freight depot (09000274)',NR%'09000274']],brick='yes'),
N('leavenworth-county-courthouse-ks','Leavenworth County Courthouse','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.31453,-94.91219,'exact','view','drive','','1911–1913',
  'The county built this Classical Revival limestone courthouse in 1911 to 1913 after a fire destroyed the courthouse of the 1870s. William Feth was the architect and J. B. Berts the builder. It still serves as the county courthouse.',
  'Cut limestone walls on a rusticated base with quoins and pediments inscribed "Leavenworth County Court House". The form says it keeps most of its original outside and inside features, though windows and doors are replaced and the second floor was reconfigured. In places the limestone faces the soft brick walls left after the fire.',
  'A Classical Revival limestone courthouse of 1913 in daily use.',
  'A working county building at 300 Walnut Street; look from the street. Visiting hours for the offices were not checked. The Register list point is some 900 metres south of the courthouse, so the pin was set from OpenStreetMap.',
  [['National Register form, Leavenworth County Courthouse (02000394)',NR%'02000394']],brick='no'),
N('carnegie-library-leavenworth-ks','Leavenworth Public Library (Carnegie Arts Center)','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.31352,-94.91394,'exact','view','drive','','1900–1902',
  'Built in 1900 with money from Andrew Carnegie, the two-storey library was the first Carnegie library in Kansas. Marshall R. Sanguinet of Fort Worth designed it. It was the public library from 1902 to 1987, an arts centre until 2012, and then passed back to the city.',
  'A Neo-Classical building of tan brick with limestone and terra cotta trim, columns, and seventeen limestone steps up to the entrance. What it is used for now was not checked, nor the inside.',
  'Kansas\'s first Carnegie library, up a flight of seventeen limestone steps.',
  'Owned by the city of Leavenworth, at 601 South 5th Street. Look from the public street; opening not checked.',
  [W('Carnegie Arts Center of Leavenworth, Kansas'),['National Register form, Leavenworth Public Library (86002010)',NR%'86002010']],brick='no'),
N('hollywood-theater-leavenworth-ks','Hollywood Theater (Leavenworth Performing Arts Center)','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.318056,-94.915,'site','open','drive','','1937–1938',
  'Leavenworth\'s only movie palace opened on July 23, 1938, to a design by Robert Boller of the Kansas City firm Boller Brothers, for the Dubinsky chain. The theatre stopped showing films in 1977 and was given to the city. It is now the Performing Arts Center, home of the River City Community Players.',
  'A stucco Art Deco front with a bold, sharply cut composition; the 1990 form describes Art Deco murals and lighting inside and a second-floor lobby built of circular forms. Whether they remain was not checked.',
  'A 1938 Art Deco movie house that is still a theatre.',
  'City-owned. You go in for a show; there are no regular visiting hours, and the dates are posted by the players. The address is given as 401 Delaware Street in the Register form and 500 Delaware Street in a listing.',
  [W('Hollywood Theater (Leavenworth, Kansas)'),['City of Leavenworth: History of the Performing Arts Center','https://www.leavenworthks.gov/parksrec/page/history-performing-arts-center'],['National Register form, Hollywood Theater (90001575)',NR%'90001575']],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('first-presbyterian-church-leavenworth-ks','First Presbyterian Church','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',39.313611,-94.913056,'site','view','drive','','1907–1909',
  'The Classical Revival church at 407 Walnut Street was built in 1907 to 1909 to designs by William Feth, with Robert and Walter Yoakum as builders. It is a red brick building (as in a photograph on Wikimedia Commons) with an Ionic portico on the Fourth Street side, light stone trim and a two-storey education wing.',
  'Six large stained glass windows in the sanctuary and many smaller ones; several are by Lamb Studios and at least one by Tiffany Studios. The columns and trim are a light buff limestone. The windows are seen from inside; whether the church opens for visitors was not checked.',
  'A Classical Revival church of 1909 with Tiffany and Lamb glass.',
  'An active congregation. Service times were not checked; look from the street unless the church invites visitors.',
  [['National Register form, First Presbyterian Church (05001515)',NR%'05001515']],brick='yes'),
N('david-brewer-house-leavenworth-ks','Justice David J. Brewer House','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.306111,-94.917222,'site','view','drive','','',
  'David Josiah Brewer, born in Smyrna in 1837 to missionary parents, came to Leavenworth on September 13, 1859. He was a Kansas district judge from 1865 to 1869 and on the state Supreme Court from 1870 to 1884, and was the first Kansan on the US Supreme Court, where he sat until he died on March 28, 1910. The house is listed for the man who lived in it.',
  'A two-storey frame house of irregular plan on a rubble stone foundation, with a small second-storey addition that looks stark beside the original. The date it was built was not found in the sources read.',
  'The frame house of the first Kansan on the US Supreme Court.',
  'Private; look from the public street at 1203 Fifth Avenue.',
  [['National Register form, David J. Brewer House (72000508)',NR%'72000508'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR]],brick='no'),
N('western-branch-soldiers-home-leavenworth-ks','Western Branch, National Home for Disabled Volunteer Soldiers','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','inst','standing',39.2786,-94.8914,'area','view','drive','','1885',
  'The Western Branch of the National Home for Disabled Volunteer Soldiers, set up for Civil War veterans, was the first Home west of the Mississippi. Wikipedia dates it to 1885 and the National Park Service to 1884. It is now the Dwight D. Eisenhower VA Medical Center and a National Historic Landmark (2011).',
  'Dozens of Victorian-era buildings: Romanesque Revival Franklin Hall, the Queen Anne Ward Memorial Building and the late Gothic Revival Chapel of the Veterans by Louis Curtiss. The original hospital, a large barracks, the stable and a hotel and theatre are gone, and all but one farm building.',
  'A Civil War soldiers\' home that still works as a veterans\' hospital.',
  'An active federal medical campus at 4101 South Fourth Street. Whether the public may walk the grounds and see the chapel was not checked; the pin marks the landmark\'s listed centre, not one building.',
  [W('Western Branch, National Home for Disabled Volunteer Soldiers'),['National Register form, Western Branch (99000456)',NR%'99000456']],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('carroll-mansion-leavenworth-ks','Carroll Mansion (Leavenworth County Historical Society)','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.306667,-94.918056,'site','open','drive','','1857–1964',
  'The house at 1128 Fifth Avenue is a museum of the Leavenworth County Historical Society. The marker says Edward Carroll bought it in 1889 and that his daughter Ella gave it to the society in 1964. Its building date is not agreed: one source says 1858 with carved woodwork added in 1882, another says it began about 1857 as a farmhouse.',
  'A large Victorian house with a red brick body and a wooden porch (as in a photograph on Wikimedia Commons), shown by volunteer-led tour. Tours are guided and admission is charged.',
  'A Victorian mansion kept as the county\'s history museum.',
  'Museum entrance and parking were not checked. Hours are from a search result quoting the society\'s contact page; its newer page says hours shift with programming and volunteers, so call ahead.',
  [['Leavenworth County Historical Society: contact and hours','https://leavenworthhistory.org/Contact.htm'],['City of Leavenworth: Carroll Mansion Museum','https://www.leavenworthks.gov/visitors/page/carroll-mansion-museum-and-historical-society']],conf='mixed',brick='yes',hours='Tuesday to Saturday 10:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., last tour 3 p.m.; call ahead (hours vary)'),
N('little-stranger-church-leavenworth-ks','Little Stranger Church and Cemetery','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',39.24514,-94.97364,'exact','view','drive','','1868–1919',
  'A congregation from Missouri put up this frame church in early 1868, on a hill at Stranger Road and Tonganoxie Road, and it closed in 1919. The National Register form calls the building vernacular, on a mortared limestone foundation, with an exterior brick chimney added in the 1920s. In 2020 it won a $90,000 Heritage Trust Fund grant, and neighbours hold pie socials to pay for repairs.',
  'Unpainted wooden clapboards, many missing, with framing showing on the sides; joined wooden doors; a few loose pews inside; and the old churchyard. The chimney was badly decayed when the form was written, before the repair work.',
  'A weathered 1868 prairie church saved by pie socials.',
  'Look from Stranger Road. Who owns it and whether it opens were not checked.',
  [['National Register form, Little Stranger Church (14001120)',NR%'14001120'],['KCUR: neighbours revive a pie social to save the church','https://www.kcur.org/arts-life/2017-06-02/to-save-their-historic-church-leavenworth-neighbors-revive-a-tasty-old-time-tradition']],brick='some'),
N('begley-bridge-millwood-ks','Begley Bridge','Millwood, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.410833,-95.123611,'area','view','drive','','1926',
  'The Leavenworth Bridge Company built this Pratt truss bridge over Stranger Creek in 1926. It is closed to cars and trucks, and the form calls it a good example of the commonest truss type in Kansas, still with much of its original design and workmanship.',
  'A single steel truss span over a wide shallow creek on two stone or concrete abutments, between two farm roads. The deck material has been replaced.',
  'A 1926 steel truss bridge, closed to traffic, over Stranger Creek.',
  'The form says the bridge is publicly owned. It lies 1.75 miles northwest of Millwood among farm roads; whether the roads and the bridge can be walked was not checked, and the surrounding land may be private. The pin is the Register list point and marks the area; the bridge itself was not seen on the aerial.',
  [['National Register form, Begley Bridge (03000373)',NR%'03000373']],brick='no'),
N('hund-school-leavenworth-ks','Hund School','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.3503,-94.9922,'site','view','drive','','1939',
  'This one-room school was built in 1939 to replace an earlier school lost to fire. The Hund family had given the land to the district for a dollar in 1882, and after the school closed the building and land went back to the family, which still owned them when the form was written. It stands about 2.75 miles west of Fort Leavenworth.',
  'A one-storey frame schoolhouse with a hipped roof and a porch on brick piers. The form says the inside keeps its original wood floors, wainscoting, glass-fronted library shelves and many school artefacts.',
  'An intact one-room school, left in a family\'s fields.',
  'On private farmland. Look from the public road; no access was found.',
  [['National Register form, Hund School (00000158)',NR%'00000158']],brick='some'),
N('abernathy-furniture-factory-leavenworth-ks','Abernathy Furniture Company Factory','Leavenworth, Kansas','ks','industry','standing',39.319722,-94.909722,'site','view','drive','','1883–1950',
  'Abernathy Furniture, among the longest-running Leavenworth industries, built its three-storey brick factory in 1883. A five-storey west building followed about 1890, with brick storage and office buildings added in 1902 and 1908. A second complex of four brick buildings, Plant K, was added half a mile north in 1926.',
  'Heavy-timber brick factory blocks on a limestone foundation on Seneca Street. What they are used for now was not checked.',
  'A five-storey brick furniture factory from the 1880s.',
  'Look from the public street at 200 to 210 Seneca Street. Ownership and use not checked.',
  [['National Register form, Abernathy Furniture Factory (04001142)',NR%'04001142'],['Kansas Historical Society: Leavenworth County register listings',KSR]],brick='yes'),
]
